// Practice quiz: Lists, dicts, sets and tuples
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/lists-dicts-sets-and-tuples

::o1 unique tags::A blog post has tags. You add tags as you go, never want the same tag twice and often ask whether a tag is present. Which collection fits best? {
~list #Not quite. A list allows the same tag twice and scans for membership.
~tuple #Not quite. A tuple cannot change, so you could not add tags.
~dict #Not quite. There is no value to store for each tag; a set is enough.
=set #Correct. A set keeps each item once and answers membership quickly.
}

::o1 dict key::You want to store the travel time between pairs of cities, looked up by the pair (from_city, to_city). What should each key be? {
=A tuple such as ("Oslo", "Bergen") #Correct. Tuples are immutable, so they can be dict keys, and they keep the order of the pair.
~A list such as ["Oslo", "Bergen"] #Not quite. Lists can change, so Python refuses them as dict keys with a TypeError.
~A set such as \{"Oslo", "Bergen"\} #Not quite. A set has no order, so Oslo to Bergen and Bergen to Oslo would look the same, and sets cannot be keys either.
~Two separate dicts #Not quite. That loses the pairing; one key per pair is simpler.
}

::o1 ordered history::You record every page a user visits, in order, including repeat visits, and keep adding to it. Which collection fits? {
~set #Not quite. A set would drop the repeat visits and their order.
~tuple #Not quite. A tuple cannot grow after it is created.
~dict #Not quite. There is no natural key to look visits up by.
=list #Correct. A list keeps order, allows repeats and can grow.
}

::o2 missing key::counts \= dict(apple\=2). Which expression returns 0 for the missing key "pear" without raising an error? {
~counts["pear"] #Not quite. Square brackets raise KeyError for a missing key.
~counts.get("pear") #Not quite. This avoids the error but returns None, not 0.
=counts.get("pear", 0) #Correct. get returns the default you pass when the key is missing.
~counts["pear", 0] #Not quite. This looks up the tuple key ("pear", 0), which is also missing, so KeyError.
}

::o2 update a dict::stock \= dict(pen\=3). After stock["pen"] \= 7 and stock["ink"] \= 1, what is stock? {
~\{'pen'\: 3, 'ink'\: 1\} #Not quite. Assignment to an existing key replaces its value.
=\{'pen'\: 7, 'ink'\: 1\} #Correct. Assigning to an existing key updates it; assigning to a new key adds it.
~\{'pen'\: 10, 'ink'\: 1\} #Not quite. Assignment replaces; it does not add 7 to 3.
~KeyError\: 'ink' #Not quite. Assigning to a missing key adds it; only reading a missing key raises KeyError.
}

::o2 remove from a list::names \= ["ana", "ben", "ana"]. What is names after names.remove("ana")? {
~['ben'] #Not quite. remove stops after the first match.
~['ana', 'ben'] #Not quite. The first ana is the one removed.
=['ben', 'ana'] #Correct. remove deletes only the first matching value.
~ValueError #Not quite. ValueError happens only when the value is not in the list.
}

::o3 unpack items::scores \= dict(ana\=8, ben\=6). What does for name, s in scores.items()\: print(name, s) print first? {
=ana 8 #Correct. items() yields (key, value) pairs in insertion order, and the loop unpacks each pair.
~('ana', 8) #Not quite. The pair is unpacked into two names, so print receives two values, not a tuple.
~ana #Not quite. s receives the value, so both are printed.
~8 ana #Not quite. The key comes first in each pair.
}

::o3 looping a dict directly::What does for k in dict(a\=1, b\=2)\: print(k) print? {
~1 then 2 #Not quite. That is what looping over .values() gives.
=a then b #Correct. Looping over a dict yields its keys.
~('a', 1) then ('b', 2) #Not quite. That is what looping over .items() gives.
~An error, because a dict is not iterable #Not quite. Dicts are iterable; they yield keys.
}
